vely to the final grade.\n\nThe written test results are normally distributed with a mean of $64$ and a standard deviation of $8$. The oral test results are normally distributed with a mean of $50$ and a standard deviation of $10$, while the results of the colloquium are normally distributed with a mean of $60$ and a standard deviation of $10$. Find the probability that a randomly selected student achieves a grade greater than $70$. You may assume that the scores on each test are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.00$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ | $0.03$ | $0.04$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1.9$ | $0.9713$ | $0.9719$ | $0.9726$ | $0.9732$ | $0.9738$ |\n| $2.0$ | $0.9772$ | $0.9778$ | $0.9783$ | $0.9788$ | $0.9793$ |\n| $2.1$ | $0.9821$ | $0.9826$ | $0.9830$ | $0.9834$ | $0.9838$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176289 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "888af95f-b68b-5b6b-8b0f-fb590f801c18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176289 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176754 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176754 #edb/ref 1061 34249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176754 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad9619d2-b27f-51af-8cff-cfe8c0a50e17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176754 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176782 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176782 #edb/ref 1061 34662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176782 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f7daf50f-b20f-5c67-8c50-cae883d3f213" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176782 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176783 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176783 #edb/ref 1061 34665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176783 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88c1b73c-099d-5661-a71a-cfe8df532c98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176783 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176839 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176839 #edb/ref 1061 37971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176839 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d9f50537-814f-5027-b3e1-f89e6fce2a3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176839 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176840 #edb/ref 1061 37972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176840 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c909f273-81c1-5ee1-9597-fb8ca7844bac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176840 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176842 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176842 #edb/ref 1061 38095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176842 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3dee677e-7717-5f55-906f-98bc32493269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176842 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177005 #edb/ref 1064 "When we form a random variable $Y$ from a linear combination of mutually independent, normally distributed random variables, the distribution of $Y$ is also normal.\n\nMore precisely, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *If $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ are mutually independent normal random variables such that*\n\n$$\nX_1 \\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma_1^2), \\qquad X_2\\sim (\\mu_2,\\sigma_2^2), \\qquad \\ldots, \\quad X_n\\sim(\\mu_n,\\sigma_n^2)\n$$\n\n> *and $a_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_n$ are real constants, then the linear combination*\n\n$$\nY=a_1X_1+a_2X_2+\\ldots+a_nX_n\n$$\n\n> *is normally distributed, where*\n> $Y\\sim N(a_1\\mu_1+a_2\\mu_2+\\ldots+a_n\\mu_n, \\: a_1^2\\sigma_1^2+a_2^2\\sigma_2^2+\\ldots+a_n^2\\sigma_n^2)$.\n\nFor example, if the random variables\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}3}), \\qquad X_2\\sim N({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}4}), \\qquad X_3\\sim N({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}1})\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, then\n\n$$\nY= \\bbox[2pt,AntiqueWhite]{3}X_1+\\bbox[2pt,AntiqueWhite]{2}X_2+\\bbox[2pt,AntiqueWhite]{4}X_3\n$$\n\nis normally distributed.\n\nLet's figure out the distribution of $Y$. We start by computing its mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= a_{1}μ_{1} + a_{2}μ_{2} + a_{3}μ_{3} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 1 + 4 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 6 + 2 + 4 \\\\\n&= 12, \\\\\nVar[Y] &= a_{1}^{2}σ_{1}^{2} + a_{2}^{2}σ_{2}^{2} + a_{3}^{2}σ_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= 3^{2} \\cdot 3 + 2^{2} \\cdot 4 + 4^{2} \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 3 + 4 \\cdot 4 + 16 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 27 + 16 + 16 \\\\\n&= 59\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY = 3X_{1} + 2X_{2} + 4X_{3} \\mid ∼N(12, 59)\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** It is vital that $X_1, X_2,\\ldots X_n$ are *mutually independent*. If they are *not*, their linear combination may *not* be normally distributed!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177005 #edb/ref 1065 5505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177005 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f7c68bf4-2275-5f3f-a765-fa30377c95a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177005 #edb/ref 1067 "Linear Combinations of Normally Distributed Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177055 #edb/ref 1064 "When we form a random variable $Y$ from a sum of **mutually independent**, normally distributed random variables, the distribution of $Y$ is also normal.\n\nMore precisely, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *If $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ are mutually independent normal random variables such that*\n> $X_1 \\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma_1^2), \\qquad X_2\\sim (\\mu_2,\\sigma_2^2), \\qquad \\ldots, \\quad X_n\\sim(\\mu_n,\\sigma_n^2)$,\n> *then the random variable*\n> $Y=X_1+X_2+\\ldots+X_n$\n> *is normally distributed, where*\n> $Y\\sim N(\\mu_1+\\mu_2+\\ldots+\\mu_n, \\: \\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2+\\ldots+\\sigma_n^2)$.\n\nFor example, if the random variables\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}3}), \\qquad X_2\\sim N({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}4}), \\qquad X_3\\sim N({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}1})\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, then\n\n$$\nY=X_1+X_2+X_3\n$$\n\nis normally distributed.\n\nLet's figure out the distribution of $Y$. We start by computing its mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= μ_{1} + μ_{2} + μ_{3} \\\\\n&= 2 + 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 4, \\\\\nVar[Y] &= σ_{1}^{2} + σ_{2}^{2} + σ_{3}^{2} \\\\\n&= 3 + 4 + 1 \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY = X_{1} + X_{2} + X_{3} \\mid ∼N(4, 8)\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** It is vital that $X_1, X_2,\\ldots X_n$ are *mutually independent*." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177055 #edb/ref 1065 6288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177055 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d3a59008-5151-5f9b-b0cb-1645b06dcef8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177055 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
